<p>
A quick look at a <a href="http://yummymelon.com/devnull/binding-the-spc-key-in-dired-and-ibuffer.html">Quick Look for Emacs</a> from Charles Choi. MacOS has the notion of a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quick_Look">Quick Look</a> to peek at a file without invoking a heavier weight application such as an editor. It&rsquo;s more than just dumping text onto the screen. It can deal with text, PDFs, HTML, various video files, ODF, Microsoft file types, and many others.
</p>
<p>
Choi likes the Quick Look functionality but, of course, would like to have it in Emacs too. It&rsquo;s not clear how to do this in general but it&rsquo;s trivially easy to do it for Dired and Ibuffer. Choi solves the problem by binding <code>dired-display-file</code> and <code>ibuffer-visit-buffer-other-window-noselect</code> to the <kbd>Space</kbd> key. These commands open the file in another window so they&rsquo;re more than just open the file as usual in Emacs.
</p>
<p>
If you like the idea of being able to peek at a file without abandoning your current window, take a look at Choi&rsquo;s post for the details on how to do it from Dired and Ibuffer windows.</p>
